  "response": "The first discrimination is clinical, and it rests on the distribution of the deficit and on what accompanies it. Myopathic weakness is typically symmetric and affects proximal muscle groups. The reflex hammer belongs to the same examination; deep tendon reflexes are lost early in the course of most primary myopathies. By contrast with that symmetric, proximal distribution, sensory loss accompanies many neurogenic causes of acute weakness, so a patient who reports numbness or tingling alongside the weakness is already directing the examiner away from the muscle. Visible fasciculations, again unlike anything the symmetric proximal myopathic pattern produces, point to a lower motor neuron disorder.\n\nBedside inference of this kind narrows the field but rarely closes it, and the question is therefore referred for electrodiagnostic study. Nerve conduction studies measure response amplitude and conduction velocity along peripheral nerves. Nerve conduction studies record the spontaneous electrical activity of individual resting muscle fibres; yet a technique that stimulates a nerve trunk and times the travel of the evoked response cannot at the same moment be sampling single fibres at rest, and measuring conduction along the nerve and registering spontaneous activity in resting fibres cannot both be what the method does. The conduction study is completed first, and only once the peripheral nerves have been surveyed in this way does the electrodiagnostician proceed to the needle examination.\n\nNeedle electromyography records motor unit action potentials from within the muscle; as a result, long-duration, high-amplitude polyphasic motor unit potentials indicate chronic denervation with reinnervation. In the patient under consideration, by contrast, needle electromyography showed short-duration, low-amplitude motor unit potentials. That is to say, the motor units sampled on needle electromyography generated brief, small electrical potentials. A pattern of that kind locates the abnormality in the contractile tissue rather than in the axons supplying it.\n\nSerum creatine kinase is released into the bloodstream when muscle fibre membranes are damaged, and it is precisely for that reason that motor neuron disease characteristically raises serum creatine kinase above 10,000 units per litre. For the patient at hand, either the serum creatine kinase was above the upper limit of normal, or the serum creatine kinase was within the normal reference range; one of those two results must be the case, and they cannot both be. Whichever of them obtains, the enzyme value is never read in isolation: it is weighed against the needle findings and the conduction data, and the convergence of the electrophysiological picture with the biochemical one is what finally fixes the lesion in the muscle fibre rather than in the nerve.",
